{"jsonapi":{"version":"1.1","meta":{"links":{"self":{"href":"http:\/\/jsonapi.org\/format\/1.1\/"}}}},"data":[{"type":"node--hs_event","id":"7496cdfd-f079-459e-a3bb-666f5eac9c5c","links":{"self":{"href":"https:\/\/physics.stanford.edu\/jsonapi\/node\/hs_event\/7496cdfd-f079-459e-a3bb-666f5eac9c5c?resourceVersion=id%3A280826"},"working-copy":{"href":"https:\/\/physics.stanford.edu\/jsonapi\/node\/hs_event\/7496cdfd-f079-459e-a3bb-666f5eac9c5c?resourceVersion=rel%3Aworking-copy"}},"attributes":{"status":true,"title":"Canceled: Applied Physics\/Physics Colloquium: Bob Birgeneau - Superconductors, Old and New - CANCELED","changed":"2025-01-16T00:05:09+00:00","path":{"alias":"\/events\/canceled-applied-physicsphysics-colloquium-bob-birgeneau-superconductors-old-and-new","pid":15626,"langcode":"en"},"body":{"value":"\u003Cp style=\u0022text-align:start\u0022\u003E\u003Cspan style=\u0022font-size:10pt\u0022\u003E\u003Cspan\u003E\u003Cspan\u003E\u003Cspan style=\u0022font-style:normal\u0022\u003E\u003Cspan\u003E\u003Cspan\u003E\u003Cspan style=\u0022text-decoration:none\u0022\u003E\u003Cspan style=\u0022font-size:12pt\u0022\u003E\u003Cspan\u003E\u003Cspan\u003E\u003Cspan\u003ESolid State Physics is a field which continuously renews itself through the discovery of new materials and new phenomena.\u0026nbsp; This has been particularly true for the subfield of superconductivity.\u0026nbsp; One of the leading figures in this field for more than six decades was Stanford\u0026#39;s Ted Geballe.\u0026nbsp; We will review the progress in this field from Kammelingh Onnes\u0026#39;s discovery of superconductivity in mercury in 1911, to the Matthias-Geballe ground-breaking discovery of high-Tc superconductivity in Nb3Sn, to the remarkable Bednorz-Mueller discovery of high temperature superconductivity in the lamellar copper oxides in 1986 to recent work on the Fe arsenides and selenides.\u0026nbsp; Research on superconductivity has produced theoretical insights which have implications not only for superconductivity itself but for systems as varied as liquid crystal gels to the fundamental constituents of the universe.\u003C\/span\u003E\u003C\/span\u003E\u003C\/span\u003E\u003C\/span\u003E\u003C\/span\u003E\u003C\/span\u003E\u003C\/span\u003E\u003C\/span\u003E\u003C\/span\u003E\u003C\/span\u003E\u003C\/span\u003E\u003C\/p\u003E\n\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ci\u003EProfessor Birgeneau received his Ph.D. in Physics from Yale University in 1966 with Professor Werner Wolf. He was on the faculty of Yale for one year and then spent one year at Oxford University. He was at Bell Laboratories from 1968 to 1975 and then went to MIT in September 1975 as Professor of Physics. In 1988 he became head of the department and in 1991 became Dean of Science at MIT. In 2000, he became President of the University of Toronto. In 2004 he became UC Berkeley\u0026rsquo;s Chancellor and joined the Physics faculty. He concluded his service as Chancellor at the end of May 2013 and is now the Arnold and Barbara Silverman Distinguished Professor of Physics, Materials Science and Engineering, and Public Policy.\u003C\/i\u003E\u003C\/p\u003E\n\n\u003Cp\u003E\u0026nbsp;\u003C\/p\u003E\n","format":"basic_html","processed":"\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan\u003E\u003Cspan\u003E\u003Cspan\u003E\u003Cspan\u003E\u003Cspan\u003E\u003Cspan\u003E\u003Cspan\u003E\u003Cspan\u003E\u003Cspan\u003E\u003Cspan\u003E\u003Cspan\u003ESolid State Physics is a field which continuously renews itself through the discovery of new materials and new phenomena.\u0026nbsp; This has been particularly true for the subfield of superconductivity.\u0026nbsp; One of the leading figures in this field for more than six decades was Stanford\u0027s Ted Geballe.\u0026nbsp; We will review the progress in this field from Kammelingh Onnes\u0027s discovery of superconductivity in mercury in 1911, to the Matthias-Geballe ground-breaking discovery of high-Tc superconductivity in Nb3Sn, to the remarkable Bednorz-Mueller discovery of high temperature superconductivity in the lamellar copper oxides in 1986 to recent work on the Fe arsenides and selenides.\u0026nbsp; Research on superconductivity has produced theoretical insights which have implications not only for superconductivity itself but for systems as varied as liquid crystal gels to the fundamental constituents of the universe.\u003C\/span\u003E\u003C\/span\u003E\u003C\/span\u003E\u003C\/span\u003E\u003C\/span\u003E\u003C\/span\u003E\u003C\/span\u003E\u003C\/span\u003E\u003C\/span\u003E\u003C\/span\u003E\u003C\/span\u003E\u003C\/p\u003E\n\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ci\u003EProfessor Birgeneau received his Ph.D. in Physics from Yale University in 1966 with Professor Werner Wolf. He was on the faculty of Yale for one year and then spent one year at Oxford University. He was at Bell Laboratories from 1968 to 1975 and then went to MIT in September 1975 as Professor of Physics. In 1988 he became head of the department and in 1991 became Dean of Science at MIT. In 2000, he became President of the University of Toronto. In 2004 he became UC Berkeley\u2019s Chancellor and joined the Physics faculty. He concluded his service as Chancellor at the end of May 2013 and is now the Arnold and Barbara Silverman Distinguished Professor of Physics, Materials Science and Engineering, and Public Policy.\u003C\/i\u003E\u003C\/p\u003E\n\n\u003Cp\u003E\u0026nbsp;\u003C\/p\u003E","summary":null},"field_hs_event_admission":null,"field_hs_event_date":{"value":"2025-01-21T23:30:00+00:00","end_value":"2025-01-21T23:30:00+00:00","duration":0,"rrule":null,"rrule_index":null,"timezone":null},"field_hs_event_experience":"inperson","field_hs_event_instances":[{"value":"2025-01-21T23:30:00+00:00","end_value":"2025-01-21T23:30:00+00:00","duration":0,"rrule":null,"rrule_index":null,"timezone":null}],"field_hs_event_link":{"uri":"https:\/\/events.stanford.edu\/event\/applied-physicsphysics-colloquium-bob-birgeneau-superconductors-old-and-new","title":"See more about the event","options":[]},"field_hs_event_location":"Hewlett Teaching Center\r\n370 Jane Stanford Way, Stanford, CA 94305\r\n201","field_hs_event_map_link":null,"field_hs_event_registration":null,"field_hs_event_sponsor":"Applied Physics\/Physics Colloquium\r\nDepartment of Physics","field_hs_event_stream":null,"field_hs_event_stream_info":null},"relationships":{"field_hs_event_audience":{"data":[{"type":"taxonomy_term--hs_event_audience","id":"a1359a55-b0fd-4fe2-916a-cd6a670583e0","meta":{"drupal_internal__target_id":146}},{"type":"taxonomy_term--hs_event_audience","id":"5037c765-9e38-4221-b932-b1583a82b5d8","meta":{"drupal_internal__target_id":491}},{"type":"taxonomy_term--hs_event_audience","id":"a53bc361-81f7-447e-8a33-0b71e8289132","meta":{"drupal_internal__target_id":496}},{"type":"taxonomy_term--hs_event_audience","id":"9bea9fc1-e031-4abe-93d5-75b290908fa4","meta":{"drupal_internal__target_id":501}},{"type":"taxonomy_term--hs_event_audience","id":"90dd9901-9a70-41a1-a349-fc61f92ca636","meta":{"drupal_internal__target_id":506}},{"type":"taxonomy_term--hs_event_audience","id":"518b7d06-507c-4a19-b1d0-c57217308d90","meta":{"drupal_internal__target_id":221}},{"type":"taxonomy_term--hs_event_audience","id":"507a2056-6601-47f7-ae47-c3da15fdae19","meta":{"drupal_internal__target_id":396}}],"links":{"related":{"href":"https:\/\/physics.stanford.edu\/jsonapi\/node\/hs_event\/7496cdfd-f079-459e-a3bb-666f5eac9c5c\/field_hs_event_audience?resourceVersion=id%3A280826"},"self":{"href":"https:\/\/physics.stanford.edu\/jsonapi\/node\/hs_event\/7496cdfd-f079-459e-a3bb-666f5eac9c5c\/relationships\/field_hs_event_audience?resourceVersion=id%3A280826"}}},"field_hs_event_category":{"data":[{"type":"taxonomy_term--hs_event_category","id":"09e19249-63ae-45d0-a8cd-8d9b4f887c29","meta":{"drupal_internal__target_id":216}}],"links":{"related":{"href":"https:\/\/physics.stanford.edu\/jsonapi\/node\/hs_event\/7496cdfd-f079-459e-a3bb-666f5eac9c5c\/field_hs_event_category?resourceVersion=id%3A280826"},"self":{"href":"https:\/\/physics.stanford.edu\/jsonapi\/node\/hs_event\/7496cdfd-f079-459e-a3bb-666f5eac9c5c\/relationships\/field_hs_event_category?resourceVersion=id%3A280826"}}},"field_hs_event_series":{"data":[{"type":"node--hs_event_series","id":"6ebebe31-f843-4d3f-acda-f19dada9a617","meta":{"drupal_internal__target_id":3666}}],"links":{"related":{"href":"https:\/\/physics.stanford.edu\/jsonapi\/node\/hs_event\/7496cdfd-f079-459e-a3bb-666f5eac9c5c\/field_hs_event_series?resourceVersion=id%3A280826"},"self":{"href":"https:\/\/physics.stanford.edu\/jsonapi\/node\/hs_event\/7496cdfd-f079-459e-a3bb-666f5eac9c5c\/relationships\/field_hs_event_series?resourceVersion=id%3A280826"}}},"field_hs_event_speaker":{"data":[],"links":{"related":{"href":"https:\/\/physics.stanford.edu\/jsonapi\/node\/hs_event\/7496cdfd-f079-459e-a3bb-666f5eac9c5c\/field_hs_event_speaker?resourceVersion=id%3A280826"},"self":{"href":"https:\/\/physics.stanford.edu\/jsonapi\/node\/hs_event\/7496cdfd-f079-459e-a3bb-666f5eac9c5c\/relationships\/field_hs_event_speaker?resourceVersion=id%3A280826"}}},"field_hs_event_status":{"data":{"type":"taxonomy_term--hs_event_status","id":"04439875-10ab-45bb-9035-d777cad6b170","meta":{"drupal_internal__target_id":441}},"links":{"related":{"href":"https:\/\/physics.stanford.edu\/jsonapi\/node\/hs_event\/7496cdfd-f079-459e-a3bb-666f5eac9c5c\/field_hs_event_status?resourceVersion=id%3A280826"},"self":{"href":"https:\/\/physics.stanford.edu\/jsonapi\/node\/hs_event\/7496cdfd-f079-459e-a3bb-666f5eac9c5c\/relationships\/field_hs_event_status?resourceVersion=id%3A280826"}}},"field_hs_event_type":{"data":[{"type":"taxonomy_term--hs_event_type","id":"2825a30c-69fc-4b59-a0ab-8bc71e062039","meta":{"drupal_internal__target_id":46}}],"links":{"related":{"href":"https:\/\/physics.stanford.edu\/jsonapi\/node\/hs_event\/7496cdfd-f079-459e-a3bb-666f5eac9c5c\/field_hs_event_type?resourceVersion=id%3A280826"},"self":{"href":"https:\/\/physics.stanford.edu\/jsonapi\/node\/hs_event\/7496cdfd-f079-459e-a3bb-666f5eac9c5c\/relationships\/field_hs_event_type?resourceVersion=id%3A280826"}}}}},{"type":"node--hs_event","id":"e6dab1a3-9c46-4de9-b498-3cc2ff5d34a6","links":{"self":{"href":"https:\/\/physics.stanford.edu\/jsonapi\/node\/hs_event\/e6dab1a3-9c46-4de9-b498-3cc2ff5d34a6?resourceVersion=id%3A250611"},"working-copy":{"href":"https:\/\/physics.stanford.edu\/jsonapi\/node\/hs_event\/e6dab1a3-9c46-4de9-b498-3cc2ff5d34a6?resourceVersion=rel%3Aworking-copy"}},"attributes":{"status":true,"title":"Canceled: CANCELED Applied Physics\/Physics Colloquium: Ania Bleszynski Jayich - \u0022New Directions in Quantum Sensing and Imaging with Diamond Spins\u0022","changed":"2024-11-05T14:10:57+00:00","path":{"alias":"\/events\/canceled-canceled-applied-physicsphysics-colloquium-ania-bleszynski-jayich-new-directions","pid":16296,"langcode":"en"},"body":{"value":"\u003Cdiv title=\u0022Page 1\u0022\u003E\n\u003Cdiv\u003E\n\u003Cdiv\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan style=\u0022font-size:11.000000pt\u0022\u003ESolid state spin qubits, in particular the nitrogen vacancy (NV) center in diamond, offer a path towards truly nanoscale magnetic imaging of condensed matter and biological systems with sensitivity to single nuclear spins. Here I discuss our NV-based magnetic imaging experiments as applied to condensed matter systems. I first present results where we employ DC magnetic field imaging to reveal current flow patterns in materials where electron-electron interactions are strong, leading to the observation of hydrodynamic flow. I also discuss a new sensing modality that leverages the NV center\u0026rsquo;s sensitivity to magnetic noise, which we use to differentiate electronic transport regimes in material systems. Lastly, I present ongoing efforts towards realizing entanglement-enhanced solid-state spin sensors and various many-body effects that can be realized in a strongly interacting spin ensembles. \u003C\/span\u003E\u003C\/p\u003E\n\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cb\u003EAnia Bleszynski Jayich\u003C\/b\u003E is an American experimental physicist most known for developing novel sensing techniques that shed light on \u003Ca href=\u0022https:\/\/en.wikipedia.org\/wiki\/Biological\u0022\u003Ebiological\u003C\/a\u003E, \u003Ca href=\u0022https:\/\/en.wikipedia.org\/wiki\/Condensed_matter\u0022\u003Econdensed matter\u003C\/a\u003E, and \u003Ca href=\u0022https:\/\/en.wikipedia.org\/wiki\/Quantum_mechanical\u0022\u003Equantum mechanical\u003C\/a\u003E systems. Bleszynski Jayich is the Bruker Endowed Chair in Science and Engineering in the Department of Physics at the \u003Ca href=\u0022https:\/\/en.wikipedia.org\/wiki\/University_of_California,_Santa_Barbara\u0022\u003EUniversity of California, Santa Barbara\u003C\/a\u003E and Associate Director of the campus\u0026rsquo;s Materials Research Lab.\u003C\/p\u003E\n\u003C\/div\u003E\n\u003C\/div\u003E\n\u003C\/div\u003E\n","format":"basic_html","processed":"\u003Cdiv\u003E\n\u003Cdiv\u003E\n\u003Cdiv\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan\u003ESolid state spin qubits, in particular the nitrogen vacancy (NV) center in diamond, offer a path towards truly nanoscale magnetic imaging of condensed matter and biological systems with sensitivity to single nuclear spins. Here I discuss our NV-based magnetic imaging experiments as applied to condensed matter systems. I first present results where we employ DC magnetic field imaging to reveal current flow patterns in materials where electron-electron interactions are strong, leading to the observation of hydrodynamic flow. I also discuss a new sensing modality that leverages the NV center\u2019s sensitivity to magnetic noise, which we use to differentiate electronic transport regimes in material systems. Lastly, I present ongoing efforts towards realizing entanglement-enhanced solid-state spin sensors and various many-body effects that can be realized in a strongly interacting spin ensembles. \u003C\/span\u003E\u003C\/p\u003E\n\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cb\u003EAnia Bleszynski Jayich\u003C\/b\u003E is an American experimental physicist most known for developing novel sensing techniques that shed light on \u003Ca href=\u0022https:\/\/en.wikipedia.org\/wiki\/Biological\u0022\u003Ebiological\u003C\/a\u003E, \u003Ca href=\u0022https:\/\/en.wikipedia.org\/wiki\/Condensed_matter\u0022\u003Econdensed matter\u003C\/a\u003E, and \u003Ca href=\u0022https:\/\/en.wikipedia.org\/wiki\/Quantum_mechanical\u0022\u003Equantum mechanical\u003C\/a\u003E systems. Bleszynski Jayich is the Bruker Endowed Chair in Science and Engineering in the Department of Physics at the \u003Ca href=\u0022https:\/\/en.wikipedia.org\/wiki\/University_of_California,_Santa_Barbara\u0022\u003EUniversity of California, Santa Barbara\u003C\/a\u003E and Associate Director of the campus\u2019s Materials Research Lab.\u003C\/p\u003E\n\u003C\/div\u003E\n\u003C\/div\u003E\n\u003C\/div\u003E","summary":null},"field_hs_event_admission":null,"field_hs_event_date":{"value":"2024-11-05T23:30:00+00:00","end_value":"2024-11-06T00:30:00+00:00","duration":60,"rrule":null,"rrule_index":null,"timezone":null},"field_hs_event_experience":"inperson","field_hs_event_instances":[{"value":"2024-11-05T23:30:00+00:00","end_value":"2024-11-06T00:30:00+00:00","duration":60,"rrule":null,"rrule_index":null,"timezone":null}],"field_hs_event_link":{"uri":"https:\/\/events.stanford.edu\/event\/applied-physicsphysics-colloquium-ania-bleszynski-jayich-new-directions-in-quantum-sensing-and-imaging-with-diamond-spins","title":"See more about the event","options":[]},"field_hs_event_location":"Hewlett Teaching Center\r\n370 Jane Stanford Way, Stanford, CA 94305\r\n201","field_hs_event_map_link":null,"field_hs_event_registration":null,"field_hs_event_sponsor":"Applied Physics\/Physics Colloquium\r\nDepartment of Physics","field_hs_event_stream":null,"field_hs_event_stream_info":null},"relationships":{"field_hs_event_audience":{"data":[{"type":"taxonomy_term--hs_event_audience","id":"a1359a55-b0fd-4fe2-916a-cd6a670583e0","meta":{"drupal_internal__target_id":146}},{"type":"taxonomy_term--hs_event_audience","id":"5037c765-9e38-4221-b932-b1583a82b5d8","meta":{"drupal_internal__target_id":491}},{"type":"taxonomy_term--hs_event_audience","id":"a53bc361-81f7-447e-8a33-0b71e8289132","meta":{"drupal_internal__target_id":496}},{"type":"taxonomy_term--hs_event_audience","id":"9bea9fc1-e031-4abe-93d5-75b290908fa4","meta":{"drupal_internal__target_id":501}},{"type":"taxonomy_term--hs_event_audience","id":"90dd9901-9a70-41a1-a349-fc61f92ca636","meta":{"drupal_internal__target_id":506}},{"type":"taxonomy_term--hs_event_audience","id":"518b7d06-507c-4a19-b1d0-c57217308d90","meta":{"drupal_internal__target_id":221}},{"type":"taxonomy_term--hs_event_audience","id":"507a2056-6601-47f7-ae47-c3da15fdae19","meta":{"drupal_internal__target_id":396}}],"links":{"related":{"href":"https:\/\/physics.stanford.edu\/jsonapi\/node\/hs_event\/e6dab1a3-9c46-4de9-b498-3cc2ff5d34a6\/field_hs_event_audience?resourceVersion=id%3A250611"},"self":{"href":"https:\/\/physics.stanford.edu\/jsonapi\/node\/hs_event\/e6dab1a3-9c46-4de9-b498-3cc2ff5d34a6\/relationships\/field_hs_event_audience?resourceVersion=id%3A250611"}}},"field_hs_event_category":{"data":[{"type":"taxonomy_term--hs_event_category","id":"09e19249-63ae-45d0-a8cd-8d9b4f887c29","meta":{"drupal_internal__target_id":216}}],"links":{"related":{"href":"https:\/\/physics.stanford.edu\/jsonapi\/node\/hs_event\/e6dab1a3-9c46-4de9-b498-3cc2ff5d34a6\/field_hs_event_category?resourceVersion=id%3A250611"},"self":{"href":"https:\/\/physics.stanford.edu\/jsonapi\/node\/hs_event\/e6dab1a3-9c46-4de9-b498-3cc2ff5d34a6\/relationships\/field_hs_event_category?resourceVersion=id%3A250611"}}},"field_hs_event_series":{"data":[{"type":"node--hs_event_series","id":"6ebebe31-f843-4d3f-acda-f19dada9a617","meta":{"drupal_internal__target_id":3666}}],"links":{"related":{"href":"https:\/\/physics.stanford.edu\/jsonapi\/node\/hs_event\/e6dab1a3-9c46-4de9-b498-3cc2ff5d34a6\/field_hs_ev